<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Brétigny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1360">1360</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Brétigny was agreed on 8 May 1360 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> and the captive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-france">John II of France</a>, ending the first phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. Edward gave up his claim to the French crown in return for full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over an enlarged Aquitaine, Calais and Ponthieu, and a ransom of three million crowns for John. It was ratified at Calais in October 1360, but war resumed in 1369.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny.jpg" alt="Treaty of Brétigny" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Richard Green · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map-_France_at_the_Treaty_of_Bretigny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1362 to 1372 the Black Prince ruled an enlarged Principality of Aquitaine held in full sovereignty under the <b>Treaty of Brétigny</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>King Edward III of England and the captive John II of France agreed the <b>Treaty of Brétigny</b>, ending the first phase of the Hundred Years' War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-8">8 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The English kings held Ponthieu as vassals of France; Edward III fought at Crécy, inside the county, in 1346, and the <b>Treaty of Brétigny</b> of 1360 gave it to him in full sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ponthieu">County of Ponthieu</a></li></ul></section>
